{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01VEEy2DVmmxAPe4q7EivBEi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`answers/03-012-how-decompose-high-level-goals-into-executable-steps.md`** covering:\n\n- **Concept:** Decomposition is an orchestration responsibility — convert vague intent into a structured DAG of atomic, verifiable sub-tasks.\n- **Mechanism:** Plan-and-Execute pattern — a dedicated planner LLM call produces a structured JSON plan (3-level hierarchy: goal → sub-goals → atomic tool calls), each step with typed inputs/outputs and a binary success criterion; executor processes one step at a time, retries, then re-plans with failure context.\n- **Planning strategy comparison:** ReAct (no upfront plan, flexible but hard to bound) vs Plan-and-Execute (explicit DAG, auditable) vs Tree of Thoughts (branching candidates, expensive) vs hardcoded DAG (regulated domains).\n- **Key pitfalls:** Relying solely on ReAct for all planning; unconstrained step granularity (\"analyze all the data\" = 100 tool calls); re-planning without passing failure context.\n\nNext item in queue: **Q13 — Chain-of-thought vs tree-of-thought vs graph planning**."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":362,"cache_read_input_tokens":62777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":362,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e6f0ae7-0c77-402d-9bba-e054e58e69ef","uuid":"0766e834-ddc0-4f9c-9050-64e8762e1b82"}
